            <ArticleTitle>T wave abnormalities identify patients with previous lateral wall myocardial infarction and circumflex artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>216-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jelectrocard.2015.11.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-0736(15)00376-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The diagnosis of previous lateral myocardial infarction is based on QRS morphology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the diagnostic role of T wave abnormalities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We studied 166 patients with known or suspected ischemic heart disease who underwent a 12-lead electrocardiogram, myocardial perfusion scintigraphy, and coronary arteriography within 90days. We excluded patients with bundle-branch block, hypertrophy, or paced rhythm.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Only one patient had a prominent R wave in V1, no patient showed lateral Q waves of necrosis. T wave amplitude in V2-V6 ≥0.6mV, and T wave amplitude in lead 1+V6 ≤0mV detected a lateral infarction (sensitivity 33 and 44%, specificity 83 and 80%). T wave amplitude in lead 1+V6 ≤0mV was the only independent predictor of infarction or LCx occlusion (AUC 0.72 and 0.74). Serum potassium values were not associated with T wave abnormalities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">T wave abnormalities identify previous lateral infarction and LCx disease.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2016. Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
